t_uuid":"05905085-7ab7-4ae0-88b5-11de2b0d1830","position":{"commit_sha":"600eba721cdec9e4a0ea613a7c6cd6a408186743","position":"1787134083221978001-05905085-7ab7-4ae0-88b5-11de2b0d1830"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"8ebf54e47c474ede4c5dc9bcb543844f7858ff1ff184a49c3e50084e6743598c","event_uuid":"be52393b-25fe-4101-bb6c-f82664502eb3","position":{"commit_sha":"985b732bf5613f2ef83da3f7e747b36abe32d72a","position":"1787134090512917001-be52393b-25fe-4101-bb6c-f82664502eb3"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":"claude-code_2-1-232_agent","claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":"03a4b6e2-5666-42d5-8aaa-0906798afc8f","comments":[],"created_at":1787022450388966001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"a8c0ae25-458a-4a66-9586-f5630be8d8ff","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"5142-4dbf-9469-4672"},{"link_uuid":"d50b0571-3551-406b-811a-4d0e6a207851","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"6633-219e-988a-42f6"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\nAfter S1, persistence has a cohesive owner but `BindingStore` still embeds forward/reverse identity transitions, pending/confirmed state, numeric-ID re-key, comment identity, absence grace, tombstones, and unretire policy. Production rich-text convergence also reaches through shallow `all_bindings()` and mutates an inner dictionary, bypassing any future policy seam.\n\n## Outcome\nExtract lifecycle decisions into a private policy owner operating on the repository original dictionaries, with the public facade retaining every existing method/signature. Add a narrow facade operation for rich-text emission state. Preserve shallow `all_bindings()` compatibility, but make the narrow operation the supported production mutation seam for the later caller cutover.\n\n## Plan\n1. Move cohesive forward/reverse, pending/confirmed, immutable-ID re-key, comment-map, absence/retirement, tombstone, suppression, and unretire call clusters into `binding_lifecycle.py`.\n2. Keep the facade authoritative: it coordinates the lifecycle owner and repository unit-of-work; no adapter or reconciler caller receives a writable repository object.\n3. Use open typed views over the same dictionaries. Unknown and legacy fields remain present and writable only through named policy operations.\n4. Add `BindingStore.note_rich_emit(local_id, wire)` (or an equivalently narrow name) that preserves the current counter/hash behavior and missing-binding fail-open result. Do not change `all_bindings()` copy depth.\n5. Preserve exact current failure choices: confirmed 404 grace/reset, moved-key repair, retired corruption fail-open, live corruption fail-closed, append-only comment identity, tombstone suppression, and explicit unretire.\n\n## Testing (RED → GREEN)\n- Direct policy tests exercise both-index transitions and reload through the real repository after each state change.\n- Table-driven tests cover duplicate bind, stale reverse cleanup, missing/corrupt forward state, three-404 retirement, 200 reset, numeric-ID move, legacy no-ID behavior, tombstone suppression/unretire, and changed/unchanged comment IDs.\n- A narrow-operation test compares rich-emission hashes/counters and save behavior to the existing production sequence.\n- Signature and shallow-query regression tests pin the facade compatibility surface.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nFacade delegates can be moved back without changing bytes or callers. This slice adds the narrow mutation operation but does not yet modify RP-04-owned apply files. No lifecycle thresholds, corruption disposition, copy-depth, stored schema, or identity-marker writer changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Given valid and corrupt forward/reverse combinations, every lifecycle operation preserves at-most-one identity and removes stale reverse entries only in the existing permitted cases (targeted single-key pops, never a guessed sweep).\n- [x] Three confirmed 404s retire, a successful read resets absence, immutable numeric-ID lookup re-keys instead of retiring, and legacy entries retain current behavior.\n- [x] Tombstoned issues suppress recreate with an alert until explicit unretire; retired corruption and alert-write failures retain their current fail-open boundaries.\n- [x] Comment IDs remain append-only/change-gated and old records without the field remain readable without rewrite.\n- [x] The narrow rich-emission operation matches current hash/counter/save behavior while missing bindings remain nonfatal.\n- [x] Existing facade signatures and shallow `all_bindings()` semantics remain unchanged; no production adapter obtains direct repository mutation access.\n\n## Correction: \"safety-aborts ambiguous double binds\" was not existing behavior\nAC1 originally required that lifecycle operations \"safety-abort ambiguous double binds\". Verified\nagainst the reviewed base: no such abort exists. `bind_confirm` unconditionally overwrites the\nforward entry's `jira_key` and pops the old reverse key only when it differs; `unbind` and the\nnumeric-ID re-key path likewise never refuse or raise. A repo-wide search for ambiguity/abort\nhandling in `binding_store.py`, `binding_repository.py` and `peer_state.py` returns nothing.\n\nAC1 is therefore corrected to the invariant that IS real and testable: at-most-one identity,\nmaintained by targeted single-key reverse pops rather than by detecting or refusing ambiguity.\nIntroducing an abort would be a behavior change, which this extraction slice is explicitly\nforbidden from making (\"no lifecycle thresholds, corruption disposition, copy-depth, stored\nschema, or identity-marker writer changes\"); if one is wanted it needs its own ticket and risk\nanalysis. The genuine safety-abort in this epic belongs to S3, where interrupted-retirement\ncompletion aborts on any tombstone/forward/reverse mismatch - that is new behavior there, and the\nterm is reserved for it to avoid two meanings across the tree.\n","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_lifecycle.py","reason":"New cohesive identity and lifecycle policy owner."},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py","reason":"Retain facade delegates and add the narrow rich-emission operation."},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_lifecycle.py","reason":"Direct lifecycle policy and both-index transition tables."},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_store_unbind.py","reason":"Corrupt/orphaned reverse cleanup regression coverage."},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_store_comment_ids.py","reason":"Append-only comment identity regression coverage."},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_move_rekey_heldout.py","reason":"Immutable numeric-ID move and facade signature oracles."},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_rich_reemit_state.py","reason":"Narrow rich-emission behavior and compatibility oracle."},{"path":"scripts/config_ownership_exceptions.py","reason":"Re-register both moved legacy config reads (os.environ.get via _env_int, and RECONCILER_ABSENT_RETIRE_GRACE) under the new binding_lifecycle.py path. The registry keys LEGACY_EXCEPTIONS on path+symbol, so moving them without re-registering makes make lint fail and blocks the commit."}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["depends_on","5142-4dbf-9469-4672"],["parent","5e64-d9cd-2dd4-4036"],["relates_to","6633-219e-988a-42f6"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"5e64-d9cd-2dd4-4036","parent_status_uuid":"05905085-7ab7-4ae0-88b5-11de2b0d1830","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":["rp02","reconciler","lifecycle","tdd"],"ticket_id":"fc1c-1b08-70c2-4afb","ticket_type":"story","title":"RP-02 S2 — Extract binding lifecycle policy and narrow mutation operations","verify_commands":[{"command":"env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" .venv/bin/pytest -q tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_lifecycle.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_store_unbind.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_store_comment_ids.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_move_rekey_heldout.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_rich_reemit_state.py","dd_id":"AC1-AC5","dd_text":"Lifecycle transitions, tombstones, comments, immutable-ID moves, and rich-emission operation"},{"command":"env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" .venv/bin/pytest -q tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_store.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_binding_move_rekey_heldout.py","dd_id":"AC6","dd_text":"Facade signatures and shallow binding query compatibility"}]},"source_event_uuids":["d50b0571-3551-406b-811a-4d0e6a207851","948093ae-3ba1-48d1-9356-76ee90e1fc63","5d813c13-b565-4b03-b266-48b8cebdb2bc","262d9466-285f-4782-9ecc-aad2b125bd08","ebdf5b40-b5d8-4f51-92c4-00e1e5a5d125","2f782e13-63a5-424f-9119-3609aa906b96","d6736575-ef3a-4cc1-b05b-cc4c608be339","815a4282-aaa4-4906-b35c-ad18568fee1c","379b1f21-74a6-460a-8b23-973ceb37729f","dd456540-8666-4649-98d9-785cc48b5ebd","05905085-7ab7-4ae0-88b5-11de2b0d1830","be52393b-25fe-4101-bb6c-f82664502eb3"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787141177659978036,"uuid":"30c99cbd-6147-41a6-ac57-259ec25008d3"}